-paychek-woman-says-coming-back-to-india-from-uk-after-4-years-not-happiest-decision-but-right-one-for-nervous-system/articleshow/133472486.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T21:17:18+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133472600,imgsize-109856.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Researchers develop 3D-printed replacements for missing pottery parts, embed sensors in them]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133459889.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Researchers at Koc University have developed a method that uses photogrammetry, 3D printing and embedded sensors to repair damaged ancient pottery. The customised 3D-printed sections can replace missing parts while monitoring temperature, humidity, pH and physical stress. Tested on maritime amphorae from Türkiye and Italy, the approach could help conservators track changes and support long-term preservation.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/researchers-developed-custom-3d-printed-replacements-for-missing-sections-of-ancient-pottery-and-embedded-sensors-inside-them-allowing-conservators-to-monitor-temperature-strain-and-environmental-damage/articleshow/133459889.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/researchers-developed-custom-3d-printed-replacements-for-missing-sections-of-ancient-pottery-and-embedded-sensors-inside-them-allowing-conservators-to-monitor-temperature-strain-and-environmental-damage/articleshow/133459889.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T20:50:13+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133463128,imgsize-106315.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[A black hole keeps tearing pieces off the same star, yet the star survives and comes back again]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133463351.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Astronomers have made fascinating observations of stars that are caught in a gravitational dance with supermassive black holes. As these stars approach, they lose material in a series of light bursts that fade over time. New research indicates that the stars' rapid initial spin is key to this phenomenon, as it limits excessive rotation during subsequent encounters with the black holes.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/some-stars-repeatedly-skim-past-supermassive-black-holes-lose-material-and-survive-scientists-now-think-their-unusually-rapid-spin-explains-why-each-returning-flare-becomes-progressively-dimmer/articleshow/133463351.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/some-stars-repeatedly-skim-past-supermassive-black-holes-lose-material-and-survive-scientists-now-think-their-unusually-rapid-spin-explains-why-each-returning-flare-becomes-progressively-dimmer/articleshow/133463351.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T20:50:03+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133463501,imgsize-323888.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Quote of the Day by Benjamin Franklin: "Well done is better than well said"]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133471191.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/quote-of-the-day-by-benjamin-franklin-well-done-is-better-than-well-said/articleshow/133471191.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/wo