 he penned half the Arthur books. With this sale, they conclude a significant chapter in their lives and have already acquired a new home in New York's Hudson Valley, ready for restoration.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1988-arthur-creator-marc-brown-bought-a-marthas-vineyard-estate-for-700000-after-37-years-and-writing-half-the-books-there-he-sold-it-for-6-4-million/articleshow/133461143.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1988-arthur-creator-marc-brown-bought-a-marthas-vineyard-estate-for-700000-after-37-years-and-writing-half-the-books-there-he-sold-it-for-6-4-million/articleshow/133461143.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-24T19:45:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133461309,imgsize-235848.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[In 1998, Costa Rica spread 12,000 tonnes of orange waste; 16 years later, forest biomass rose 176%]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133466780.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In 1998, about 12,000 metric tonnes of orange peels and pulp were spread over a degraded pasture in Costa Rica as part of an unusual forest restoration experiment. Sixteen years later, researchers found that the site had developed into a dense forest, with richer soil, greater tree diversity and a 176% increase in aboveground biomass.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-1998-costa-rica-spread-12000-tonnes-of-orange-waste-over-degraded-pasture-16-years-later-tree-diversity-had-tripled-and-forest-biomass-was-176-higher/articleshow/133466780.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-1998-costa-rica-spread-12000-tonnes-of-orange-waste-over-degraded-pastu